The Purpose of Static Members in OOPIn Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), the static keyword is used to define variables
or methods that belong to the class itself rather than to any specific instance (object) created from that class.
This means that a static member is shared globally by all instances, and memory for it is allocated only once when the class is loaded.
Key Advantages of Using Static Members:
1. Memory EfficiencyDescription: 
Instead of allocating new memory for every single object created, only one copy of the static member exists in memory, 
which is shared by all instances.
Example: If you have a Student class where every student goes to the same university, 
storing the universityName as a regular variable would waste memory by duplicating the same text for thousands of students. 
Making it static ensures it is stored only once.
2. Shared Data and CommunicationDescription: 
Static variables act as a shared communication bridge between all objects of the same class. 
Any change made to a static variable by one object is instantly visible to all other objects.
Example: A common use case is creating an object counter. By incrementing a static variable inside the class constructor, 
you can accurately track exactly how many instances of that class have been created across the entire program.
3. Access Without InstantiationDescription: 
Static methods can be called directly using the class name without the need to create or instantiate an object using the new keyword.
Example: Utility methods, such as mathematical functions (e.g., Math.sqrt() in Java or C#), are defined as static 
because they perform operations purely based on their inputs and do not require object data to function.
